course: everyday
lesson: 378
title: Maddie the tomboy
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Maddie climbed the tallest horse chestnut in the park and refused to come down for tea. Her mum stood at the bottom shouting, «Fish fingers are going cold!» Maddie shouted back, «I can see the whole town!» She came down in the end, with conkers in every pocket and a rip in her trousers. Her gran laughed: «I was just as wild. I climbed the church wall in my Sunday best and the vicar nearly fainted.»
explain: For her cousin's wedding, Maddie flatly refused to wear a dress. After a long row, Mum found her a small navy suit, and Maddie looked so sharp that three aunties asked where it was from. At school a girl called Sophie said Maddie «isn't a proper girl». Maddie said, «I'm a girl. This is what I'm like,» and went off to play football with the boys on her street, who always picked her first.
explain: Some kids call her «Mad Maddie» because she is rough and fierce. That fierceness has a good side — she is brave, loyal and never boring — and a hard side: it gets her into fights. Her best friend is Lily, the calm «princess» with the long ponytail, and nobody can work out how they are friends. They just are: Lily calms Maddie down, and Maddie makes Lily laugh.
explain: Maddie wanted to join the boxing club, and her dad said no, worried she'd get hurt. Then she asked why girls never got a game in the big street football at the rec. There is no rule against it; it's just habit, and habits can be changed by asking.
fact: A tomboy is a girl who likes things people often think of as «for boys», like climbing, football or rough games.
fact: There is no single right way to be a girl or a boy.
fact: Girls and women play football, rugby and boxing, and many play for their countries.
fact: A sport with rules, a coach and safety gear is different from a fight in the street.
fact: Parents sometimes say no to a sport because they are worried, and talking calmly can change their mind.
fact: Friends can be very different from each other and still be close.
fact: A nickname like «Mad Maddie» can be meant as a joke but still sting.
fact: Many grandparents and parents were wild as children too.
fact: Groups that leave someone out «because that's how it's always been» can be changed by asking politely.
exercise: Why wouldn't Maddie come down from the tree for tea? | She loved seeing the whole town from the top | She was stuck and couldn't move | She was hiding up there from her gran after a row | She was cross about fish fingers
exercise: What did Maddie's gran say about her own childhood? | She was just as wild and climbed the church wall | She was always quiet and stayed indoors | She never climbed anything | She was scared of trees
exercise: What did Maddie wear to her cousin's wedding? | A small navy suit | A long pink party dress | Her red football kit | Her school uniform
exercise: How did the aunties react to Maddie's suit? | Three of them asked where it was from | They sent her home to change | They laughed at her | They didn't let her in
exercise: Sophie said Maddie «isn't a proper girl». Which answer is true? | There is no single right way to be a girl | Proper girls must wear dresses | Tree-climbing girls aren't proper girls | Only girls with plaits are proper girls
exercise: Why do the boys on her street pick Maddie first for football? | She is good at football | She is the only one with a ball | She makes them all do it | Her dad pays them to do it
exercise: How might Maddie feel when she is called «Mad Maddie»? | Partly proud and partly stung | Only happy, every time | Nothing at all | Confused about her own name
exercise: What is the good side of Maddie's fierceness? | She is brave, loyal and never boring | She wins every single fight she ever gets into | She frightens the teachers | She never has to say sorry
exercise: What is the hard side of Maddie's fierceness? | It gets her into fights | It makes her bad at football | It makes her shy at school | It stops her climbing trees
exercise: How are Maddie and Lily good for each other? | Lily calms Maddie, Maddie makes Lily laugh | They both love dresses | They both like fighting | They never talk to each other
exercise: Why did Maddie's dad say no to the boxing club? | He was worried she would get hurt | He thinks girls aren't allowed to box | The club was too far away from home | He wanted her to play the piano instead
exercise: How is a boxing club different from a street fight? | It has rules, a coach and safety gear | There is no difference at all between them | Nobody ever gets tired | You can hit anyone you like, as long as it's in the gym
exercise: What wise step could Maddie take about the boxing club? | Talk calmly with her dad and maybe visit the club together | Go behind his back and join the club anyway without telling him | Stop speaking to him for a month | Start fights to prove she's tough
exercise: Why are girls not playing in the big street football at the rec? | Habit, not any real rule | There is a law against it | Girls can't kick a ball far | The rec belongs to the boys
exercise: What brave thing could Maddie do about the rec football? | Ask politely if she and other girls can play | Take the ball off the boys and run off with it across the field | Cry until they let her | Pick a fight with the biggest boy
exercise: What kind of courage does Maddie show at the wedding? | Being herself when others expect something else | Climbing over the church wall to get the ball back | Fighting her cousin | Hiding in the car park
test: «maddie's not a proper girl, she don't even have plaits lol». Right or wrong? | Wrong, there's no one right way to be a girl | Right, girls need plaits | Right, girls can't play football | Wrong, because she's actually a boy who just has a girl's name
test: «girls can't box, that's just a fact». Is it? | No, many girls and women box in clubs | Yes, it's against the rules | Yes, girls just aren't strong enough to box | Only girls over 30 can box
test: «get down from that tree NOW, tea's on the table!!» Why did Maddie take ages? | She loved the view from the top | She had fallen asleep up there in the sun | She didn't like her name | She'd lost her shoe
test: «the rec lot say no girls, them's the rules». Are they? | No, it's just a habit, not a real rule | Yes, it's the law in this country | Yes, the council says so on a sign | Only on Sundays
test: «how r Maddie and Lily even mates, one's a tomboy one's a princess??» What's true? | Very different friends can be very close | They must be pretending to be friends | They will fall out soon, because they are too different | They only meet to fight
test: Gran: «I was just as wild at your age». What is Gran telling Maddie? | Being wild as a kid is nothing new | She wants Maddie to fight | She never liked climbing anything at all as a girl | Maddie should stay indoors
test: «she wore a SUIT to the wedding, weirdo». What's the kindest true reply? | She looked great and it's her choice | Suits are only for boys | She'll get into trouble with the bride for it | Weddings ban suits for girls
test: «dad said no boxing, so that's that forever». Is it? | Not always, talking calmly can change a parent's mind | Yes, parents never change their minds | Yes, unless she sulks for long enough to make him give in | No, she can just go without telling him
test: «Mad Maddie lol». Can a joke nickname hurt? | Yes, even a joke can sting | No, nicknames never hurt | Only if it's said in maths | Only if it's spelled wrong
test: «boxing club's the same as scrapping in the street innit». Is it? | No, a club has rules, coaches and safety gear | Yes, exactly the same | Yes, but with a bit less shouting | No, the club is much more dangerous than a fight in the street
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term «tomboy»? | A girl disposed towards pursuits commonly, though erroneously, regarded as masculine | A girl who is not truly a girl | A boy who prefers quiet pursuits | A girl who is prohibited by her parents from wearing dresses at any time
exam: Identify the principal distinction between participation in a supervised boxing club and engagement in a street fight. | The former is governed by rules, coaching and protective equipment | The former permits unlimited striking of any person | The latter is safer by reason of its considerably shorter duration | There exists no material distinction whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the basis upon which girls have customarily been excluded from a particular recreational football game. | Habit, as distinct from any rule or law | A statute of the realm | A regulation issued by the council | The physical incapacity of girls to play
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning friendship between children of markedly differing temperaments. | Such friendships may be close and mutually beneficial | Such friendships are invariably short-lived | Such friendships are prohibited in schools | Such friendships require both of the parties to change their natures entirely
